title: Isometric tilemaps
intro: A tilemap's cells can now be diamonds: painted in the Scene view, standing up as blocks, and sorted in depth with what walks among them.
project: Empty | Iso Garden
write: Tiles/iso.png | isometric-tilemaps/iso.png

chapter: Diamonds | Shape: Isometric
create: Tilemap
set: Transform.Position = 0, 0, 0
pickimage: Tilemap.Image = Tiles/iso.png
set: Tilemap.Columns = 3
set: Tilemap.Tint = #FFFFFF
set: Tilemap.Tile Size = 2
say: Here's a tileset drawn for an isometric view: a patch of grass, a stone block and a crate, each sitting on a diamond.
set: Tilemap.Shape = Isometric
explain: field:Tilemap.Shape | Shape | A tilemap's new Shape setting turns its cells into diamonds, twice as wide as they're tall. The grid runs down to the right and down to the left from the map's position.
look: 0, -3, 4
click: tiles:Rectangle
click: tiles:0
scenedrag: 0, -0.5 > 0, -5.5
expecttile: Tilemap 5, 5 = 0
expecttile: Tilemap 0, 5 = 0
explain: world:-6, 0, 6, -6 | Painting follows the diamonds | The brush, the rectangle and the fill all paint the diamond under the pointer, and the outline and the map's edge are diamonds too.

chapter: Standing up | Tile Height, on a layer of walls
set: Tilemap.Tile Height = 1
explain: field:Tilemap.TileHeight | Tile Height | Tile Height says how tall each tile's picture is, in tile widths. A half is a flat diamond, a floor. One is a square picture: a block standing on its diamond, as tall again.
click: tiles:layer:+
rename: Walls
click: tiles:Rectangle
click: tiles:1
scenedrag: 3, -2 > 0, -3.5
click: tiles:Brush
click: tiles:2
sceneclick: -2, -2.5
expecttile: Walls 3, 3 = 1
expecttile: Walls 1, 3 = 2
explain: world:-4, 0, 5, -5 | Back to front | Walls and crates go on a layer of their own. The tiles are drawn back to front, a diagonal at a time, so each block overlaps the ones behind it.

chapter: In front and behind | Sort By Y
deselect
create: Sprite
rename: Player
set: Sprite.Size = 0.6, 1.2
set: Sprite.Tint = #E04848
set: Transform.Position = 0, -2.5, 0
explain: entity:Player | Behind a wall | The player stands just behind the block in the middle, but for now it's drawn over the whole wall layer.
select: Walls
set: Tilemap.Sort By Y = true
explain: component:Tilemap | Sort By Y | Turn on Sort By Y on the walls, and each diagonal row of blocks is drawn in its own place among the sprites. The Inspector reminds us the camera needs Sort By Y as well.
select: Main Camera
set: Camera.Sort By Y = true
select: Player
explain: world:-2, -1, 2, -4 | Hidden behind it | Now the block in front hides the player, the way it should.
set: Transform.Position = 2, -3.15, 0
explain: world:0, -1, 4, -4 | And in front | Stepping out in front of the wall, the player draws over it. The floor sorts as one, under everything standing on it.
play
wait: 1.5
say: The game draws it just the same. Isometric maps don't collide yet: walking and blocking come next, then an Isometric template.
stop
